<R7RG92vEowPP1H0T9kZIW9W> a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Commandment 03: Remember to keep holy the Lord’s Day." ;
    rdfs:subClassOf <RDJPdWE32ZF0O3QeYJi5uc> ;
    skos:definition "Remember to Keep Holy the Lord's Day represents the third commandment mandating weekly cessation from work to dedicate time for worship, rest, and spiritual renewal. This decree establishes the Sabbath rhythm as covenant sign, memorial of creation and liberation, and anticipation of eternal rest, requiring both physical rest and sacred assembly."^^xsd:string ;
    skos:example "Catholic social teaching cited this commandment when advocating for workers' rights to weekly rest against industrial exploitation."^^xsd:string,
        "Early Christians shifted Sabbath observance from Saturday to Sunday, commemorating Christ's resurrection as the new creation day."^^xsd:string,
        "Medieval canon law specified Sunday Mass obligation and prohibited servile work, markets, and legal proceedings."^^xsd:string,
        "Worker advocacy groups invoked this commandment when opposing seven-day retail schedules and gig economy demands."^^xsd:string ;
    skos:note """Remember the Sabbath Command (Exodus 20:8)
Six Days Work Allowance (Exodus 20:9)
Seventh Day Rest Requirement (Exodus 20:10)
Universal Rest Scope (family, servants, animals, strangers) (Exodus 20:10)
Creation Grounding (Exodus 20:11)
Deuteronomic Liberation Grounding (Deuteronomy 5:15)
Death Penalty for Sabbath Violation (Exodus 31:14-15)
Jesus's Sabbath Healings (Mark 3:1-6)
Jesus's "Lord of the Sabbath" Declaration (Mark 2:27-28)
Apostolic Sunday Worship Transition (Acts 20:7, Revelation 1:10)
Constantine's Sunday Rest Legislation (321 CE)"""^^xsd:string ;
    skos:prefLabel "Third Commandment"^^xsd:string .

<R7RIiGEhbNy3YC5PH9ohXMp> a owl:Class ;
