        "Truth-Telling Commandment"^^xsd:string,
        "Witness Integrity Commandment"^^xsd:string ;
    skos:definition "You Shall Not Bear False Witness Against Your Neighbor represents the eighth commandment prohibiting lying and deception, especially in legal testimony that harms another's reputation or freedom. This decree protects truth, justice, and personal honor by forbidding perjury, slander, gossip, and all false speech that damages others, establishing truthfulness as essential to community trust and integrity."^^xsd:string ;
    skos:example "Confessors have helped penitents understand how this commandment required repairing reputational damage caused by slander or detraction."^^xsd:string,
        "Moral theologians have distinguished between lies (always wrong), mental reservation (sometimes permissible), and equivocation in life-threatening situations."^^xsd:string,
        "Spiritual directors have applied this commandment to self-deception and rationalization that obscured honest self-examination."^^xsd:string,
        "Teenagers in confirmation classes have questioned whether this commandment permitted white lies to avoid hurting others' feelings."^^xsd:string ;
    skos:note """Prohibition Statement (Exodus 20:16)
Deuteronomic Restatement (Deuteronomy 5:20)
False Witness Penalty Matching Intended Harm (Deuteronomy 19:16-21)
Multiple Witness Requirement (Deuteronomy 19:15)
Naboth's Vineyard False Testimony (1 Kings 21:1-16)
Jesus's Trial False Witnesses (Matthew 26:59-61)
Ananias and Sapphira's Deception (Acts 5:1-11)
Colossians' Truth Command (Colossians 3:9)
Satan as Father of Lies (John 8:44)"""^^xsd:string ;
    skos:prefLabel "Eighth Commandment"^^xsd:string .

