<R9eL9sN4ttYlKVTFZnGsq2F> a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Commandment 10: You shall not covet your neighbor’s goods." ;
    rdfs:subClassOf <RDJPdWE32ZF0O3QeYJi5uc> ;
    skos:altLabel "Envy Commandment"^^xsd:string,
        "Greed Commandment"^^xsd:string,
        "Material Covetousness Commandment"^^xsd:string ;
    skos:definition "You Shall Not Covet Your Neighbor's Goods represents the tenth commandment's prohibiting envious desire for another's possessions, addressing interior greed and materialism. This decree regulates heart attitudes toward property and wealth, forbidding the disordered longing that breeds discontent, envy, and potential theft while establishing interior detachment and gratitude as moral imperatives."^^xsd:string ;
    skos:example "Confessors have helped penitents recognize how this commandment addressed root desires preceding theft or fraud."^^xsd:string,
        "Moral theologians have distinguished between admiring another's possessions and coveting them with resentful envy."^^xsd:string,
        "Spiritual directors have applied this commandment to consumerism and comparison culture that bred chronic dissatisfaction."^^xsd:string ;
    skos:note """Prohibition Statement (Exodus 20:17)
Comprehensive Property List (house, servant, ox, donkey, anything)
Deuteronomic Restatement (Deuteronomy 5:21)
Achan's Covetousness and Theft (Joshua 7:20-21)
Ahab's Vineyard Coveting (1 Kings 21:1-4)
Paul's Covetousness Struggle (Romans 7:7-8)
Colossians' Greed as Idolatry (Colossians 3:5)
Hebrews' Contentment Exhortation (Hebrews 13:5)
James's Envy Warning (James 3:14-16)"""^^xsd:string ;
    skos:prefLabel "Tenth Commandment"^^xsd:string .

