<R9xIs5buoetnLhyWxPQ50FE> a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Armistice" ;
    rdfs:subClassOf [ a owl:Restriction ;
            owl:onProperty rdfs:seeAlso ;
            owl:someValuesFrom <Agreements> ],
        <R9X6xDAYYSYwCeCqMHt5G75> ;
    skos:altLabel "War-Ending Ceasefire"^^xsd:string ;
    skos:definition "An armistice means a formal agreement between opposing combatants to suspend hostilities, often across an entire theater of conflict and on stated terms. An armistice ends active fighting but does not itself settle the conflict’s underlying legal and political issues unless the parties also conclude a peace settlement."^^xsd:string ;
    skos:example "The armistice created a joint commission to investigate alleged violations."^^xsd:string,
        "The armistice established a demarcation line and required withdrawal from designated zones."^^xsd:string,
        "The armistice required prisoner exchanges on a fixed schedule."^^xsd:string,
        "The parties signed an armistice that halted fighting while peace talks continued."^^xsd:string .

<R9xJ00xJgSL0NvQLfQv6HA8> a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Healing of the High Priest’s Servant" ;
    rdfs:subClassOf <R8hw8N5f6CCS519HZccaPdl> ;
    skos:altLabel "Healing of Malchus"^^xsd:string,
        "Miracle at Jesus’ Arrest"^^xsd:string,
        "Restoration of the Servant’s Ear"^^xsd:string ;
    skos:definition "Healing of the High Priest’s Servant means the miraculous healing performed by Jesus when he restored the ear of the high priest’s servant wounded during Jesus’ arrest. Healing of the High Priest’s Servant reveals Christ’s mercy toward his enemies and his rejection of violence at the moment of his Passion."^^xsd:string ;
    skos:example "Act of Mercy Toward an Enemy"^^xsd:string,
        "Christian theology interprets the healing of the high priest’s servant as a sign of mercy amid betrayal and violence."^^xsd:string,
        "The Gospel of Luke records Jesus healing the ear of the high priest’s servant during his arrest."^^xsd:string .

