<RILtMHLpDM8luT3w9ni6LK> a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Irrelevant Evidence Objection" ;
    dc:source "Fed. R. Evid. 401",
        "Fed. R. Evid. 402" ;
    rdfs:subClassOf <R9Bk5v9AAJmhb4WcgIQjy29> ;
    skos:altLabel "Irrelevant",
        "No Probative Value Objection",
        "Non-Pertinent Evidence Objection",
        "Objection to Inapplicable Evidence",
        "Unrelated Evidence Objection" ;
    skos:definition "An Irrelevant Evidence Objection is raised when a party in a legal proceeding believes that the evidence being presented is not related to the issues at hand or does not have any probative value. This objection aims to prevent the introduction of extraneous information that could distract or mislead the decision-maker." ;
    skos:prefLabel "Immaterial Objection" .

<RILuqAMxcqNJm8vHgoOo7a> a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Visa for Families of Skilled Workers" ;
    rdfs:subClassOf [ a owl:Restriction ;
            owl:onProperty rdfs:seeAlso ;
            owl:someValuesFrom <R76UTCrg8DDzlFkZCPjrdqe> ],
        <RBVXMCXoBW3Ef1c3VUuU84a> ;
    skos:altLabel "H-4 Visa"@en-us ;
    skos:definition "A Visa for Families of Skilled Workers, such as the U.S. H-4 Visa, is a category of visa that allows the immediate family members (spouse and dependent children) of skilled workers with certain types of visas to accompany them to the host country. These family members are often granted the right to reside in the country and, in some cases, may be eligible to apply for work authorization." .

<RIOjneXO96zMhlYgGHiovK> a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Major Weight-Bearing Joint Surgery" ;
    dc:source <https://www.ssa.gov/disability/professionals/bluebook/1.00-Musculoskeletal-Adult.htm#1_17> ;
    rdfs:subClassOf <RpiyvcnyPAZ5hygNt0zO3j> ;
    skos:definition "Reconstructive surgery or surgical arthrodesis of a major weight-bearing joint involves surgical procedures aimed at repairing or stabilizing large joints that support significant body weight, such as the hip, knee, or ankle. This type of surgery is performed to restore function, alleviate pain, and improve stability in joints severely affected by injury, disease, or degeneration." ;
    skos:prefLabel "Reconstructive Surgery or Surgical arthrodesis of a Major Weight-bearing Joint" .

<RIPGUOqov5ujn1dMUQF7sZ> a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Suburban Market Segment" ;
    rdfs:subClassOf <RVu433XojPQPseoDXhidgO> ;
    skos:definition "The suburban market segment refers to a group of consumers or businesses located in the suburbs, areas that are typically situated on the outskirts of urban centers and characterized by a mix of residential and commercial zones. This segment is distinguished by its unique lifestyle and consumption patterns, often blending urban convenience with a more spacious and family-oriented environment." .

<RIQnXfIDi8YStyeDYkefmC> a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Equal Protection Claim" ;
    rdfs:subClassOf <RDr6DXAcukDYZgOiiRN8qtf> ;
    skos:altLabel "Gleichheitsschutzklage"@de-de,
        "Equal Protection Claim"@en-gb,
        "Reclamo de Protección Igualitaria"@es-es,
        "Reclamo de Protección Igualitaria"@es-mx,
        "Recours en protection de l'égalité"@fr-fr,
        "תביעת הגנה שווה"@he-il,
        "समान अधिकार दावा"@hi-in,
        "平等保護請求"@ja-jp,
        "Reivindicação de proteção igualitária"@pt-br,
        "平等保护索赔"@zh-cn ;
    skos:definition "An equal protection claim is a legal action initiated by an individual or group alleging that their Fourteenth Amendment right to equal protection under the law has been violated by the government or a government actor. The Equal Protection Clause of the Fourteenth Amendment prohibits the government from treating individuals or groups differently based on their race, gender, national origin, or other protected categories. An equal protection claim may arise in various contexts, such as when a government enacts a law or policy that treats certain individuals or groups unfairly or unequally, or when a government actor engages in discriminatory conduct. Examples of unequal treatment can include unequal enforcement of laws, unequal access to public services or benefits, or unequal treatment in the criminal justice system." .

