<RbnnN7AqBQb8UNyadsds7z> a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Athanasian Creed" ;
    dc:source <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Athanasian_Creed> ;
    rdfs:subClassOf <RyTfHxUQPVMUQrtIt1Mycz> ;
    skos:altLabel "Quicumque Vult"^^xsd:string ;
    skos:definition "Athanasian Creed is a doctrinal creed that articulates the Church’s teaching on the Trinity and the Incarnation with precise theological definitions. It emphasizes the equality and unity of the divine persons and the true divinity and humanity of Jesus Christ as necessary elements of orthodox faith."^^xsd:string ;
    skos:example "\"For, when all the rest who sympathised with us were divided into three parties, and many were faltering in their conception of the Son, and still more in that of the Holy Ghost, (a point on which to be only slightly in error was to be orthodox) and few indeed were sound upon both points, he was the first and only one, or with the concurrence of but a few, to venture to confess in writing, with entire clearness and distinctness, the Unity of Godhead and Essence of the Three Persons, and thus to attain in later days, under the influence of inspiration, to the same faith in regard to the Holy Ghost, as had been bestowed at an earlier time on most of the Fathers in regard to the Son. This confession, a truly royal and magnificent gift, he presented to the Emperor, opposing to the unwritten innovation, a written account of the orthodox faith, so that an emperor might be overcome by an emperor, reason by reason, treatise by treatise.\" (Oration 21, p. 33)"^^xsd:string,
        "On Trinity Sunday, the community studied excerpts from the Athanasian Creed."^^xsd:string,
        "The catechist referenced the Athanasian Creed to clarify orthodox teaching on Christ’s two natures."^^xsd:string .

