    skos:definition "Testimonial Objectives refer to the various arguments, purposes, or exceptions regarding testimonial evidence. Examples include out-of-court statements that might be admitted into evidence. These testimonial objectives can include items such as present sense impression, excited utterance, and situations where the declarant is unavailable to testify in person." .

